@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "From SiriKit to App Intents") {
        Write "complete a memo" with SiriKit to feel how much work it took,
        then rewrite the same action with App Intents. Finally, see how an app that used SiriKit custom intents
        moves to App Intents without breaking its existing users' shortcuts.

        Sources: `docs/apple-doc/sirikit/sirikit.md`,
        [SiriKit](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/sirikit),
        [CustomIntentMigratedAppIntent](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/customintentmigratedappintent)
    }

    @Section(title: "A SiriKit intent handler") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            With SiriKit, you first define the custom intent's schema in an Xcode `.intentdefinition` file.
            Xcode generates a `CompleteMemoIntent` class and a `CompleteMemoIntentHandling` protocol from it,
            and you write a handler that conforms to that protocol inside an Intents app extension.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a class that conforms to the generated `CompleteMemoIntentHandling`.
                This type usually lived in a separate Intents app extension target, not in the app itself.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Write the first stage, `resolveMemo`. If `intent.memo` is empty, return `.needsValue()`
                so that Siri asks back "Which memo?". Every parameter you add
                needs one more `resolve…` method like this.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler-02.swift", previousFile: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Write the second stage, `confirm`. Check that the intent is ready to run and return `.ready`.
                Even when there was nothing to check, you couldn't skip this stage.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler-03.swift", previousFile: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                The third stage, `handle`, finally completes the memo. Return the result by passing
                a `CompleteMemoIntentResponse` to the completion handler. Exposing a single action
                took a schema definition, an extension target and three methods.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler-04.swift", previousFile: "CompleteMemoIntentHandler-03.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                On top of that, SiriKit required you to add the Siri capability to the app
                and ask the person for permission to use Siri with `INPreferences.requestSiriAuthorization`.
                App Intents has no such permission prompt.

                @Code(name: "SiriAuthorization.swift", file: "SiriAuthorization-01.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Rewrite it with App Intents") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Write the same "complete a memo" with App Intents. You create no schema file and no extension;
            you only put one struct in the app itself (in this repository, the `ios/Library` package).
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a struct that conforms to `AppIntent` and give it a `title`.
                This `title` becomes the action name shown in the Shortcuts app and Spotlight.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntent.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Declare the input with `@Parameter`. Its type is `MemoEntity`, which you build in chapter 2.
                You don't write anything like SiriKit's `resolveMemo`. When the value is missing,
                the system asks back using the `MemoEntity` suggestions.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntent.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntent-02.swift", previousFile: "CompleteMemoIntent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Write the work in `perform()`. You can use async/await, and you report failure by `throw`ing.
                When you need a check like SiriKit's `confirm`, call `requestConfirmation`
                (covered in chapter 4) inside `perform()`.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntent.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntent-03.swift", previousFile: "CompleteMemoIntent-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                The compiler generates the metadata that Siri, Shortcuts and Spotlight need from this code
                at build time. As soon as the app is installed, "Complete Memo" appears in the Shortcuts app,
                with no registration call in advance.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntent.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntent-04.swift", previousFile: "CompleteMemoIntent-03.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Carry over existing SiriKit users") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            SiriKit, Intents and IntentsUI are still supported as legacy frameworks,
            but only App Intents integrates with Apple Intelligence and Siri AI.
            An app that already ships SiriKit custom intents has to migrate without breaking
            the shortcuts its users made.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Conform to `CustomIntentMigratedAppIntent` and set `intentClassName` to
                the class name of the SiriKit intent being replaced. Existing shortcuts and donations
                that used the old intent keep working with the new App Intent.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntent.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntent-05.swift", previousFile: "CompleteMemoIntent-04.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                This repository never used SiriKit, so it doesn't need this migration when you implement it.
                A new app can start with App Intents alone. Leave a comment that says so.

                @Code(name: "CompleteMemoIntent.swift", file: "CompleteMemoIntent-06.swift", previousFile: "CompleteMemoIntent-05.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Next steps") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Once this makes sense, copy the prompt below and paste it into Claude Code.
            It builds no feature; it only confirms the decision not to use SiriKit and checks that the base project
            is ready for the implementation in the following chapters.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                When you've understood the chapter, paste this into Claude Code. It confirms the approach and makes sure
                the project builds, tests and launches.

                @Code(name: "prompt.txt", file: "01-prompt.txt")
            }
        }
    }
}
